.Dt LIBFSIMG 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm libfsimg
.Nd bounded filesystem image readers, writers and safe extraction
.Sh LIBRARY
Filesystem image library
.Pq Fl l Ns fsimg ;
a static link also needs the system zlib
.Pq Fl l Ns z ,
which
.Pa libfsimg.pc
lists
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fsimg.h
.Ft void
.Fn fsimg_options_init "fsimg_options_t *options"
.Ft fsimg_status_t
.Fn fsimg_ctx_new "const fsimg_options_t *options" "fsimg_ctx_t **context" "fsimg_error_t *error"
.Ft fsimg_status_t
.Fn fsimg_io_from_fd "fsimg_ctx_t *context" "int fd" "unsigned flags" "fsimg_io_t **io" "fsimg_error_t *error"
.Ft fsimg_status_t
.Fn fsimg_io_slice "fsimg_io_t *parent" "uint64_t offset" "uint64_t length" "fsimg_io_t **slice" "fsimg_error_t *error"
.Ft fsimg_status_t
.Fn fsimg_reader_open "fsimg_ctx_t *context" "fsimg_io_t *io" "const fsimg_read_options_t *options" "fsimg_reader_t **reader" "fsimg_error_t *error"
.Ft fsimg_status_t
.Fn fsimg_writer_new "fsimg_ctx_t *context" "const char *format" "const fsimg_write_options_t *options" "fsimg_writer_t **writer" "fsimg_error_t *error"
.Ft fsimg_status_t
.Fn fsimg_image_open "fsimg_ctx_t *context" "fsimg_io_t *io" "const fsimg_image_options_t *options" "fsimg_image_t **image" "fsimg_error_t *error"
.Ft fsimg_status_t
.Fn fsimg_image_open_stream "fsimg_ctx_t *context" "int fd" "const fsimg_image_options_t *options" "fsimg_image_t **image" "fsimg_error_t *error"
.Ft fsimg_status_t
.Fn fsimg_writer_add_manifest "fsimg_writer_t *writer" "int manifest_fd" "int contents_fd" "fsimg_error_t *error"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The public C11 API supports FAT12, FAT16, FAT32, ordinary one-FAT exFAT,
classic QNX6 Power-Safe volumes in either byte order, and QNX4 volumes,
and reads QNX ImageFS images in either byte order.
A format without full verification has no verify capability, and
.Fn fsimg_verify
fails on it with
.Dv FSIMG_EUNSUPPORTED
before doing any work.
It requires only libc, host OS APIs and the system zlib, and is includable
from C++.
It neither prints diagnostics, exits, changes process-global policy nor
sandboxes its embedding application.
.Pp
Initialize option and callback structures with their matching init function.
Their size and version must match the header.
Zero resource values choose documented defaults.
All dynamic library allocations and owned descriptors share one resource
context.
Payload copying, metadata, allocation indexes and sorting obey separate memory,
scratch, spool, work and traversal limits.
A handle is used serially; independent contexts may be used concurrently.
Callbacks cannot re-enter an active context.
.Sh OWNERSHIP
FD adapters duplicate borrowed regular-file descriptors, preserving position
and shared flags.
Read authority requires an O_RDONLY descriptor.
Slices borrow their parent, have checked fixed bounds and cannot resize it.
Callback tables are copied; callback state, buffers and backing storage remain
caller-owned and stable for the documented lifetime.
The optional next_zero_range callback, which requires read_at, reports the
next range at or after an offset that reads as zero, within the object and
making progress; the library fails an answer outside that shape with
FSIMG_EIO and otherwise encodes the reported range as zeros without reading
it, so a callback that reports data as zero puts zeros in the output.
Zeroing never consults it: an object the library did not create empty is
zeroed with zero_range or written zeros in full.
A read-only descriptor object answers from the host's hole report instead,
after syncing its file once, and never for the chunk holding its last byte,
which is read so that a file truncated after the lookup fails the read; a
lookup that finds the file shorter than the object fails with
FSIMG_ESOURCE_CHANGED, and a failed sync turns the report off for that
object.
Its queries seek the file offset the descriptor shares with the caller's
and restore it before returning, so nothing else, another context included,
may use that offset while an operation reads the object.
A writable descriptor object answers nothing.
No library handle owns an embedding application's memory implicitly.
.Pp
Free readers and writers before their context.
Free a slice before its parent and keep reader inputs alive until reader
destruction.
Destructors accept NULL.
A writer owns its immutable plan; plan and entry snapshots are borrowed.
Iteration invalidates the previous current-entry snapshot.
.Sh READERS
.Fn fsimg_reader_next
enumerates depth-first in on-disk order and returns FSIMG_DONE at complete EOF.
Directory preparation validates case/alias ambiguity before exposing children.
A QNX4, QNX6 or ImageFS entry whose name QNX accepts but lies outside the
profile,
strict UTF-8 without control bytes or DEL, is omitted: it and its subtree are
never returned, no selection can name it, and each omitted subtree root is
counted in the context statistics' omitted_entries.
.Fn fsimg_reader_read_at
reads only the current regular file or symbolic link, clips to EOF and returns
confirmed bytes separately from status.
A symbolic link's content is its target, which is never resolved.
Confirmed bytes can accompany failure and do not imply complete content.
Uninitialized logical tails read as zero without exposing underlying
cluster bytes.
.Pp
.Fn fsimg_reader_get_info
reports header scope, not complete filesystem verification.
A format that records a mount point reports it in mountpoint, with
FSIMG_VOLUME_MOUNTPOINT set in valid.
Format-specific values follow in details, detail_count of at most
FSIMG_VOLUME_DETAILS_MAX entries in the driver's order, each a name of
printable ASCII of up to 23 bytes and a 64-bit value.
An ImageFS reports its mount flags, boot and script inode references, chain
address, record counts by inode flag nibble, and the numbers of implicit
directories, shadowed records, omitted subtrees and references to omitted
entries.
An ImageFS parent without a record reads as a directory with mode 0755,
owner 0 and time 0, and of records with the same path only the last is
returned.
.Fn fsimg_verify
requires an untouched reader and consumes traversal.
FSIMG_VERIFY_PAYLOAD also reads initialized payload bytes.
Its report is complete only after the entire requested scope succeeds.
Verification also checks omitted subtrees, reading their payload; its files,
directories and logical bytes count only entries iteration would return.
No reader modifies or repairs an input image.
.Pp
Every path the library emits other than an entry's own path is rendered:
the path of an error and of the loss callback.
A backslash is written
.Ql \e\e ,
so such a path holds
.Ql \e\e
where the entry path holds
.Ql \e ,
and in an omitted name every byte outside 0x20 to 0x7e is written
.Ql \exNN
in lowercase hexadecimal.
The rendering is reversible.
An error path too long for its field keeps its tail, is cut only between whole
escapes and characters, and sets path_truncated; the loss callback's path is
never truncated.
.Sh WRITERS
Collect host trees, manifests, explicit zero sources or one callback
provider before planning.
Paths are absolute UTF-8 image paths.
Entries are regular files, directories, symbolic links, whose size is the
target length of 1 through 1024 bytes and whose provider or host source
supplies the target, and FIFOs, which have neither size nor source.
Metadata validity bits cover creation, modification, access and change times,
attributes, owner (uid and gid) and the permission bits 07777.
Host scanning records symbolic links without following them, rejects
special files and passes over the context's scratch directory, which it
refuses as a root; it crosses mount points beneath a root.
FSIMG_WRITE_IMPORT_POSIX also records each object's owner and permission bits.
A host root is a directory, whose tree populates its image path, or a
regular file, which is the one file at a non-root image path.
A directory that several roots or entries supply becomes one directory
holding all their children; its metadata comes from a scanned record before
a programmatic one, and from the first-added root among scanned records.
Programmatic directories of one path with no scanned record of that path
merge with each other only when they carry identical metadata.
Any other repeated path, a discarded directory that requires metadata, or
programmatic directories of one path whose metadata differ, with no scanned
record of that path to outrank them, fail planning with
FSIMG_EINVAL naming the path.
.Fn fsimg_writer_add_manifest
imports an
.Xr mtree 5
manifest as one more root whose entries rank as scanned records at its
position among the roots.
.Fa manifest_fd
is an
.Dv O_RDONLY
regular file, borrowed and read through a duplicate from offset zero to its
length when the call begins; it must be unchanged when the parse ends.
.Fa contents_fd
is an
.Dv O_RDONLY
directory, duplicated, beneath which a file without generated content takes
its bytes from its
.Cm contents
path or its own path, or \-1.
The directory becomes a root only when an entry first needs host bytes;
an entry that needs them without one fails.
Contents paths are walked one component at a time without following links
and never leave the directory; a link on the path, or a directory or
special file at its end, fails with
.Dv FSIMG_EUNSUPPORTED .
Owner, mode and modification time come from the manifest's keywords, never
from a content file.
The
.Cm fsimg.content
keyword selects zero, seeded or positional generated content instead, with
.Cm fsimg.seed
and
.Cm fsimg.holes .
A generated file's
.Cm sha256digest
is checked by
.Fn fsimg_writer_plan
once the image and the output limit have accepted every size, so no more
bytes are hashed than the planned volume holds, and a mismatch fails
planning with
.Dv FSIMG_EINVAL ;
a host file's is checked as emission copies it, and a mismatch fails
emission with
.Dv FSIMG_ESOURCE_CHANGED
naming the path.
An input failure, and a generated digest mismatch, names the manifest line
by byte offset in the error's offset and by number at the start of its
message, and the entry's image path.
Host identity checks detect ordinary metadata-visible changes;
hostile concurrent mutation requires immutable inputs or a snapshot.
Provider content and state remain borrowed until writer destruction.
Opaque callback sources and sinks must use disjoint storage.
.Pp
.Fn fsimg_writer_plan
seals collection, validates names and metadata, and fixes deterministic
geometry.
Plans report logical, initialized, allocated, metadata and free byte quantities
separately, plus metadata-loss counts and observed scratch use.
Reproducible mode fixes timestamps, identifiers, ordering and padding.
Optional losses follow the explicit allow-loss policy; required losses fail.
A field the selected format does not store, as its capability record states,
is an omission under the same policy, counted once per field.
An entry kind the format does not store always fails planning, as does a
symbolic link whose target is longer than the record's
max_link_target_bytes, which is zero exactly when the format stores no
links.
A format record whose max_label_bytes is zero has no volume label and accepts
only a NULL label.
The settings byte_order, volume_uuid, inode_capacity and reserve_percent
select the byte order, the UUID in textual byte order, the inode capacity
and the reserved-block percentage (default 3) of a format whose record's
write_settings mask names them; writer construction rejects a non-default
value for any other format with FSIMG_EINVAL.
QNX6 names all four, and zero inode_capacity selects its native ratio
raised to the number of entries.
QNX4 names inode_capacity alone, the
.Pa .inodes
slot count, where zero selects 128 slots raised to the slots the names
need.
QNX6 and QNX4 store no volume_serial and ignore it.
Time bases distinguish UTC instants, known offsets and unknown-zone calendar
values.
.Pp
.Fn fsimg_writer_emit
attempts emission once to a bounded sink.
A raw sink may retain partial bytes after failure and must be discarded.
.Fn fsimg_writer_write_at
takes a trusted parent directory descriptor and a single new basename.
It plans before image creation and publishes without replacement after durable
finalization.
Its staging directory beside the basename, like that of every file output,
is a private directory, as described under
.Sx EXTRACTION .
Known source aliases are rejected before output mutation.
Inspect output_state and cleanup_error before any recovery decision.
Retained staging paths are relative to the supplied parent.
.Sh LAYERS
The format argument of
.Fn fsimg_writer_new
is a stack naming the filesystem first, such as
.Ql fat32/gpt :
a gpt or mbr scheme writes a disk holding the volume as its one
partition at 1 MiB, with every byte outside the tables and the volume
zero.
The layers member of the write options points to an
.Vt fsimg_layer_options_t ,
initialized with
.Fn fsimg_layer_options_init ,
whose fields hold the scheme's type, name, identifiers, active flag, disk
length and GPT sector size; an unset disk or partition identifier is
derived from the epoch under FSIMG_WRITE_REPRODUCIBLE and is otherwise
FSIMG_EINVAL naming its key.
The plan reports the disk and the partition as an opened image would.
.Pp
.Fn fsimg_writer_output
writes to an
.Vt fsimg_sink_t
that
.Fn fsimg_sink_init
makes empty for its kind, with every descriptor -1: a new file published
beneath a parent directory, a positional I/O object, or a stream descriptor
written in order.
A stack naming a container, such as
.Ql fat32/gpt/dmg ,
a compression, or a stream sink needs the sink's raw_fd: an
exclusively created, empty, unlinked read/write regular file, of which the
caller keeps no other descriptor or mapping, the writer
emits the raw disk into before one sequential transcode writes the
container, then gzip around it, or copies the disk, to the sink; it is
adopted and closed on every outcome.
The write options' compression names one registered compression, such as
.Ql gzip ,
or is empty for none; a name the registry lacks is FSIMG_EINVAL and one
this build cannot write FSIMG_EUNSUPPORTED.
gzip writes one member with a fixed header: no name,
comment or header CRC, OS 3, extra flags by level, and as its time the
epoch under FSIMG_WRITE_REPRODUCIBLE or the host clock otherwise, zero when
outside the field's 32 bits.
The layer options add the DMG compression, level, chunk size and segment
ID, the VHD alignment and unique ID, the sparse image block size and CRC32
chunk, and the gzip level, 1 to 9; the plan adds the compression, the
output bound, the container padding and the zlib version the output
depends on, and the output limit must hold the raw disk and that bound
together.
A stream has no publication: a failure after its first byte reports
FSIMG_OUTPUT_INCOMPLETE.
A failure of a layer, on open, verification or a container read, is
FSIMG_OP_IMAGE with the layer's name in error.path.
.Fn fsimg_image_wrap
puts a raw volume into a scheme,
.Fn fsimg_image_unwrap
writes the bytes an opened image's selection names, raw, and
.Fn fsimg_image_compose
builds a disk from several volumes, one partition each; each compresses
its output with the write options' compression.
They copy the disk once, read each input once, and refuse an output that
shares storage with an input before writing.
.Pp
.Fn fsimg_image_open
peels a DMG, fixed VHD or Android sparse image container before anything
else reads the input: the container probes run on the input's first bytes
and last 512 bytes, or only the one the stack's container slot forces, and
a claimed container's tables are validated completely, so a damaged or
unsupported container fails the open with FSIMG_ECORRUPT or
FSIMG_EUNSUPPORTED and is never read as a raw disk.
Selections then address the container's disk, served through a cache of
four decoded chunks of at most 16 MiB each, allocated at open within the
memory limit; decoded bytes count as work.
.Fn fsimg_image_get_info
reports the container's name, chunk count, chunk encodings and identifier;
.Fn fsimg_image_verify
checks its checksums and, under FSIMG_VERIFY_PAYLOAD, decodes every chunk.
.Fn fsimg_layer_next
reports each container's chunk encodings and the zlib version it uses.
.Pp
Before the container, the open decides whether the input is compressed:
alone, a compression's signature makes it compressed; when an
uncompressed layer's probe also claims the first bytes, the open fails as
ambiguous unless the image options name any part of the stack, which reads
it as uncompressed, or name the compression, which runs only its probe, so
an input it does not claim fails with FSIMG_EFORMAT.
Image options whose size
.Fn fsimg_image_options_init
did not set are refused before spool_fd is examined, as a sink is before
its raw_fd.
A compressed input is decoded, member after member with each member's
CRC32 and length checked, into the spool: the image options' spool_fd, an
exclusively created, empty, unlinked read/write regular file, of which the
caller keeps no other descriptor or mapping, the open
adopts and closes on every outcome, and refuses with FSIMG_EINVAL naming
the spool in error.path otherwise.
.Fn fsimg_image_open_stream
reads a non-seekable descriptor, such as a pipe, in order, decoding it or
copying it unchanged into the spool; container probes, which need the last
512 bytes, do not run on a stream.
Each step's logical length is reserved against the spool_bytes limit, 64
GiB by default, before the spool grows, so a decompression bomb fails with
FSIMG_ELIMIT and FSIMG_RESOURCE_SPOOL with the file still within the limit;
zero blocks stay holes.
Compressed bytes read and decoded bytes produced count as work, for a file
and a stream alike, so the work limit bounds a decode as it bounds a
container's.
The statistics report spool_bytes, spool_peak and the spool's allocated
spool_physical_bytes; image information reports the compression and
file_bytes, the length of the file the layers were peeled from, input or
spool.
.Sh EXTRACTION
.Fn fsimg_extract_at
requires an untouched reader and a fresh destination basename beneath a trusted
parent.
It creates private directories/files with descriptor-relative no-follow
operations,
never merges, overwrites, restores privileged metadata or follows links.
.Pp
Every directory the library creates to write into, the destination of
.Fn fsimg_extract_at
and the staging directory of a file output such as
.Fn fsimg_writer_write_at ,
is created with mode 0700, reopened without following links and checked on
that descriptor: it must be owned by the effective user, have no group or
other write permission, carry no non-trivial access control list of any type
the host reports, and be empty.
A parent whose new directories fail the check, such as one with an
inheritable ACL entry, or whose writers put another directory under the new
name before the reopen, fails the call with FSIMG_EINVAL and a message
naming what the parent does to new directories.
On macOS, a parent whose inheritable ACL gives another user the right to
change new directories' permissions or ACL leaves a race the check does
not close; do not create the library's directories beneath one.
A refused directory is removed again while the name still holds the
directory the library opened, owned by the effective user and empty;
otherwise, unless the name now holds another object or nothing, or the
removal finds the directory populated, which holds nothing the library
wrote, the error's path names it as possibly retained.
A removal that fails for another reason, such as EACCES from an ACL that
denies deletion, or EPERM, leaves the directory's contents uninspected, so it is
reported even if it is populated.
An extraction whose destination cannot be created or is refused leaves the
reader untouched.
In capability mode the parent descriptor needs the rights these queries
take, such as reading ACLs and removing directories, besides creating
them.
The check reads the directory through one transient extra descriptor, which
counts against max_open_files.
It trusts the owner and permissions the filesystem reports, so a
filesystem that does not enforce them defeats it.
For example, on a volume that ignores ownership, such as an external macOS
APFS or HFS+ volume by default, a directory passes while every local user
can write it.
Separately, on a filesystem that ignores the modes requested for new files, such as
Linux vfat with a permissive fmask, a directory can pass while the files
created in it are writable by others.
Keep output and scratch off such filesystems when other users are not
trusted.
.Pp
Logical lengths including holes count against output limits.
Failure after creation can leave private incomplete output.
The default restores no host timestamps.
Owners, permission bits and change times are never restored.
A symbolic link, FIFO or other kind fails extraction unless
FSIMG_EXTRACT_SKIP_UNSUPPORTED omits it and counts it in the context
statistics' skipped_entries.
An omitted entry within the selected scope fails extraction the same way
unless FSIMG_EXTRACT_SKIP_UNSUPPORTED leaves it and its subtree out; it is
counted in omitted_entries, never in skipped_entries.
An entry name the host filesystem cannot represent, such as one longer than
its component limit, fails with FSIMG_EUNSUPPORTED.
Explicit timestamp restoration requires a time-basis policy and does not
change the embedding process's sandbox.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Checked source examples are installed beneath
.Pa share/fsimg/examples :
create-tree.c, provider.c, read-slice.c and extract.c.
They demonstrate initialization, borrowing, bounded slices, partial-read
handling,
safe publication, extraction and cleanup.
Build with the installed libfsimg pkg-config metadata or direct compiler
include/library paths.
.Sh ERRORS
Functions return structured status values and optionally fill fsimg_error_t.
Errors distinguish argument/state failures, corruption, unsupported features,
host I/O, resource exhaustion, source changes, cancellation, existing targets
and uncertain durability.
Per-call error storage is caller-owned; there is no global error buffer.
Publication outcomes are absent, incomplete, published or uncertain.
Cancellation after naming the output but before required directory sync
completion
retains a published output with uncertain durability.
Never retry a published or uncertain outcome blindly.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fsimg 1
